  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Soil Health in Suriname; An international network and a monitoring framework</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Agriculture, together with mining, is one of the main drivers of soil health decrease in Suriname. Soil health can be defined as the continued capacity of soil to function as a vital living ecosystem, that sustains plants, animals and humans. Years of inappropriate and unsustainable land use and soil management are exacerbating the problem of soil degradation in Suriname. Intensive agricultural practices, including slash-and-burn practices, result in a negative impact on soil functions and ecosystem services such as productivity, contaminant mitigation and water purification. The current project aims at establishing an international collaborative network that will assess and interpret currently available digital soil information (data) for problem analysis, to identify needs for soil information for upscaling/extrapolation. Also, the most degraded sites and main problems for Surinamese agriculture will be defined, and recommendations for concrete follow-up actions will be discussed and defined. The project will focus on agricultural areas where slash-and-burn practices are applied and will focus on the most degraded regions in the country. This information will be used to develop a soil health monitoring framework that will help researchers, practitioners, farmers and ultimately policymakers to understand and adapt soil management in order to maintain and/or enhance soil functioning and tackle soil degradation. Through the use of on-field visits, lectures and interactive workshops, an intensive knowledge exchange and collaboration between the Netherlands and Suriname will be initiated.</narrative>
  </description>
